<!-- SECTION 3 -->
<div class="section-eyebrow">The bypass correction</div>
<h2 class="article-section-title">Lombok handles 21.6 percent of China's crude. That traffic is at risk, but it is not trapped.</h2>

<p class="prose">The Lombok Strait, 400 km east of Malacca, is navigable by laden VLCCs. It handles deep-draft vessels excluded from Malacca's 25-metre depth limit and adds approximately four days to a voyage from the Indian Ocean to Chinese ports. At current VLCC day rates, that represents roughly $268,000 in voyage cost overrun per transit.</p>

<p class="prose">The CDM shows that 21.6 percent of China's seaborne crude currently routes via Lombok rather than Malacca. That traffic represents $70.6 billion in annual trade value and it is already using the bypass. If Malacca closes, this 21.6 percent continues to arrive. A Malacca closure does not affect cargo already on the Lombok path. Including it in a Malacca dependency figure overstates the corridor's leverage by the full 21.6 percentage points.</p>

